The markdown export flattens line breaks inside a table cell, so a ten-line cell and a one-line cell are indistinguishable in the snapshot. Replacing a whole cell from that single-line source collapses the list, and the post-push re-export flattens the damage identically, so Phase 6 verification cannot detect it. This is the only corruption the engine cannot catch after the fact. Editing a substring within one line of the cell stays safe and must not be refused.

The escaped-cell case is the one the hand-written fixtures originally missed: the export escapes characters the document does not contain, so an operator reading the live cell types the unescaped form, a raw-only comparison finds no cell match, and the occurrence counter then matches it on its de-escaped pass — letting a whole-cell replace through unguarded. Found by running the guard against a real export.
